---
title: Hard constraints
---

# Hard constraints

Non-negotiable rules for any change in this codebase. A build that
violates one of these is wrong even if it renders correctly.

## Never

- Never add a runtime dependency — no new gems, no npm packages, no
  CDN scripts. The set is closed: Rails, Turbo, Stimulus, basecoat
  (CSS + JS from the `basecoat-css` npm package), Tailwind.
- Never write raw markup for something a partial covers — check the
  [decision table](decision_table) first.
- Never make a partial that only aliases a class string onto one
  element. If its whole body is one tag plus a `variant`-to-class
  map, the class is the API — write the markup inline.
- Never pre-extract a component for UI that exists on one page. Inline
  it; extract on the third instance, or sooner only if it bundles
  behaviour, state, or an accessibility contract a caller could get
  wrong.
- Never hard-code a colour, inline a `style=` attribute, or invent a
  spacing value off the scale.
- Never re-implement behaviour the platform ships: no JS focus
  traps (`<dialog>`), no JS accordions (`<details>`), no custom
  form controls over native inputs.
- Never stack overlays, open one on page load, or put critical
  information only in a toast.
- Never disable a control to communicate unavailability outside a
  form; omit it.
- Never commit a component change without its preview and spec, and
  never delete or skip an existing test.
- Never add params, variants or options no current page consumes.

## Always

- Always read a partial's `<%%# %>` doc comment before rendering it;
  pass locals by name, take defaults unless there is a reason.
- Always start a new piece of UI inline in its page. Promote it to a
  partial when a third page needs the same thing, or immediately when
  it carries behaviour or an a11y contract worth bundling — a
  component groups markup, styling and behaviour, not styling alone.
- Always use basecoat's shipped classes before writing CSS; new CSS
  goes in `app/assets/tailwind/components/<name>.css`, registered in
  `application.css`.
- Always use colour tokens by meaning (`--critical` for harm,
  `--destructive` for delete actions) and verify both themes.
- Always keep navigation state in the URL and validation on the
  server.
- Always write interface text per the *Content* page: sentence case,
  verb-first buttons, what-happened + what-to-do errors.
- Always run the relevant specs, then `bin/rails lint`, before
  declaring work done — both must pass, in that order.
